module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Syntax

let content(): String =
  """# Syntax

## Basics
  let x = 5
  let add(a: Int64, b: Int64): Int64 = a + b
  if cond then a else b
  // comment

## Numbers
  1, 1s, 1s64   Int64          1u, 1u64   UInt64
  1s8 1s16 1s32 1s128          1u8 1u16 1u32 1u128
  1z            BigInt         1.5, 1e5, 1.5f64   Float64
  1.5f32        Float32        19.99dec   Decimal (no exponent)

`z` is for ℤ, the integers: `1z` is a BigInt, with no size limit. The suffix
decides the type, and both operands of `+` must have the same one: `1 + 1s32` is
an error, so write `1s32 + 1s32`. Values print with their suffix except Int64 and
Float64, so output pastes back as the same value. A type written `Int` is Int64 and
`Float` is Float64; Float32 is always written out.

## Statements
  Stdlib.printLine("start")        // a line before the last must return Unit
  let _ = Stdlib.List.length(xs)   // to drop any other value, bind it to _
  total                            // the last line is the block's value

This holds in every block: a function body, a lambda, an if or match branch,
and the top level of a script. When the code has more than one statement,
`dark run` and `eval` type-check it first and run none of it if a line drops
a value or has any other definite type error, even in a branch that would not
be taken. A save reports the same error, and `commit` refuses it.

## Functions
  let greet(name: String): String = $"Hello, {name}"
  let twice(f: Int64 -> Int64, x: Int64): Int64 = f(f(x))   // one argument: A -> B
  let both(f: (Int64, Int64) -> Int64): Int64 = f(1, 2)     // several: (A, B) -> C

Nested functions are supported in a function body. Each parameter needs a
type, as at the top level; the return type is optional. Neither is checked
yet: both are dropped when the nested fn becomes a local lambda.
  let outer(n: Int64): Int64 =
    let double(x: Int64): Int64 = x * 2
    double(n)

A package item named `__name` is an internal helper for its module, not public
API. Listings, search and completion hide it unless given `--include-internal`;
it is still called, viewed and depended on by its full name.

## Constructors
  type Shape = | Dot | Circle(Float64) | Rect(Float64, Float64)
  Shape.Circle(1.0)     // fields in parentheses, like a call's arguments
  Shape.Rect(1.0, 2.0) // several fields
  Shape.Dot            // no fields

Use `Type.Case(...)` to construct an enum value, for example `Shape.Circle(1.0)`.
In match patterns, use the case name alone: `| Circle(r) -> r`.

`Some`, `None`, `Ok`, and `Error` are exceptions: they can also be constructed
without the type name.

## Match
  match x with
  | Some(0) | None -> 0         // or-pattern
  | Some(v) when v > 9 -> 9     // guard
  | Some(v) -> v
`docs pattern-matching` has every pattern form, `as` included.

## Result and Option unwrap
  let next(input: String): Stdlib.Result.Result<Int64, Stdlib.Int64.ParseError> =
    let number = Stdlib.Int64.parse(input)?
    Ok(number + 1)

`value?` extracts the value from Ok/Some, or immediately returns Error/None
from the function or lambda containing it. Write Ok/Some for the success result.
Unwrapping an Option requires an Option return; unwrapping a Result requires a
Result return with the same error type. The success types may differ.

Put `?` right after the thing you want to unwrap. `f(x)?` unwraps the result
of the call. In `a + f(x)?` only `f(x)` gets unwrapped; to unwrap a whole
pipeline or operator expression, put it in parentheses first:
`(value |> transform)?`, `(a + b)?`.
The operand runs once; an early return skips remaining work without undoing
completed effects. `?` requires a function or lambda.

## Pipe
  (Stdlib.List.checkedRange(0, 10) |> Stdlib.Result.withDefault([])) |> Stdlib.List.map(fn)

You don't need parentheses around a call, literal or operator expression on
the left. You do need them around an `if` or `match`: `if c then a else b |> f`
only pipes `b`, as if you had written `if c then a else (b |> f)`. Write
`(if c then a else b) |> f` instead.

## Lists (comma separator)
  [1, 2, 3]

## Dicts and records
  Dict { "a": 1, "b": 2 }
  type Point = { x: Int64, y: Int64 }
  Point { x = 1, y = 2 }
  { point with x = 3, y = 4 }

Fields and entries use commas or newlines. Semicolons are a parse error.
A key only known at run time replaces an earlier equal key, as
`Stdlib.Dict.set` does; two keys written as the same constant are reported
by the type checker (`dark typecheck`).

## Strings
  "hello" + " world"     // concat
  $"value: {x}"          // interpolation
  "tab\tthen newline\n"  // escapes: `docs escapes` has them all

## Lambda
  fun x -> x + 1
  fun(acc, x) -> acc + x     // two parameters, like a call's arguments
  fun((a, b)) -> a + b       // one tuple parameter, taken apart

## Unit
  ()     // value
  Unit   // type

## Traits (docs traits)
  trait Area<'a> = let area(s: 'a): Float64
  impl Area for Circle = let area(c: Circle): Float64 = ...
  impl Display for Point = let display(p: Point): String = ...
  let f<'a: Display>(v: 'a): String = v.display

## Printing multiple lines
  // When printing 3+ lines, prefer printLines over repeated printLine:
  [ "line 1"
    "line 2"
    "line 3"
  ] |> Stdlib.printLines

## Keywords
  """
  + Stdlib.String.join(LanguageTools.Parser.keywords, " ")
  + """

These can't be names. To use one anyway, put it in backticks:
  let `type` = "json"
Where only a name can go, a keyword needs no backticks:
  metadata.type
  Metadata { type = "json" }
A name can't hold spaces: write hello_world or helloWorld, not `hello world`.
A few more words are special only in one place, and free everywhere else:
`module` and `test` at the start of a declaration, `for` in `impl X for T`,
`as` in a pattern, `Dict` and `List` before `<`. `def` is not reserved."""
